 spindalis was chosen as a national  bird  let me see it's called reynita mora  in puerto rico or reynamora  or sigua another name i found and in  english it used to be called  striped headed tanager for obvious  reason it has a lot of stripes  but now we know that it's not really  close related to tanagers  it's a unique caribbean lineage and  there are four species  on different islands and this one is  endemic to puerto rico  then moving over to jamaica in jamaica  this  gorgeous hummingbird was chosen as a  national bird  it's called by jamaican's doctor bird or  dr hummingbird  i'm not i couldn't find a good  explanation for this name but  the male has spectacularly long  tail and in flight must be a pleasure to  see  and strangely enough uh birds in the  eastern part of the country have black  bells and the rest of the country  red bulls this one here is a bit faded  but it used to be  a red-billed bird and female is without  this  ornamental feathers in the tail and a  little bit smaller  but it's still a common bird not  endangered  then uh let's maybe look at oh yeah  maybe  here the parrots so three different  caribbean  countries uh insular countries chose  parrots for their national birds  let's maybe look at this one first this  is the largest of the  member of the genus amazona imperial  parrot appropriately named and this  great bird is endemic to the island of  dominica  and there it's called it's called cicero  it's an endangered species there are  only fewer than 200 individuals left in  the wild  and not too many in captivity so  this one has to be watched and cared for  managed carefully  the neighboring island of saint lucia  chose  their own amazon totally differently  colored  but still quite colorful and also  it's not very common let's see that the  name  they chose for it local name is uh  jacquard  or jack what i'm i think it's jacques  and in this case there are fewer than  500 birds left in the wild  and both of these birds are in the  mountains in the interior of the island  so hopefully inaccessible and hopefully  the habit will be preserved  and then the third of these amazons of  the caribbean  this parrot here called saint vincent  parrot on  the island of saint vincent in the  grenadines has  two different color morphs green and  yellow and  here we have a representative of the  green  and these birds are a little bit more  common not as endangered as the other  two  i mentioned before but still it's a  relatively small  published in small island and a little  lower in this case not  in the mountains then  we go to island of aruba  where interestingly two birds were  chosen for the national bird first it  was the  choco locally named borrowing owl  subspecies endemic to  aruba and the second one more recently  this little parakeet brown throated  parakeet which  there is called prikichi  uh probably onomatopoeic probably after  the call  and i guess the reason for this switch  was to bring attention to this bird that  maybe is in  more need of conservation and that's why  the country brings  attention to it and finally i know not  finally but  we are traveling further south this is  now trinidad  and two birds here because trinidad  island has the scarlet ibis chosen as  their national bird  and the island of tobago  has this chachalaka this is  one of the guanz chicken-like birds  and this one is called roofos tailed  rafikauda in latin because of this nice  rufus-tipped tail and also the belly is  just not colored  this specimen is maybe not the nicest  the ibis is a common sight  throughout trinidad and it's a marsh  bird  nesting in big colonies and often seen  in flocks  traveling from the feeding grounds to  the to their nests  and i then will move to these two big  birds  so the island of barbados in the country  of barbados let me see here  chose the brown pelican as their  national bird  so here maybe actually this view this is  a species that is  pretty widely distributed not only in  caribbean islands but also in  southern united states and it's a  colonial nestor that  i mean everybody talks about egg  thinning and  endangerment of the raptors but this  bird also suffered quite a bit in 1950s  1970s from the  use of ddt and from  the pesticides and egg thinning  and finally here is the bird  chosen by the country of antigua and  barbuda  and this is the magnificent magnificent  frigate bird  sometimes called man of war this is one  of the seabirds  famous for its behavior of stealing  food from other birds can steal fish or  some other oh but they can also catch  their own fish they can put  up for example snap flying fish  from there and this bird carries a male  in life  this would be a bright red inflatable  sack and the males  on the breeding grounds would inflate  this to attract females females don't  have this  inflatable sac and i will end by saying  that  one cool kind of record that this bird  also  beats is that uh frigate bird's skeleton  is  lighter than the feathers if you  actually plucked all the feathers and  combine them  the skeleton is so pneumatized and so  light fused  that it's lighter so that's a pretty  good choice  also for the national bird

